					<h2 class="post-title">Smartphone Holder: Why you need a smartphone holder</h2>
					<div class="post-meta">
						<ul>
							<li>
								<i class="tf-ion-ios-calendar"></i> 12, February 2020
							</li>
							<li>
								<i class="tf-ion-android-person"></i> POSTED BY ADMIN
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="post-content post-excerpt">
						<p> Smartphone Holder allows you to improve the quality of your recordings – for both audio and video.<br>
						When you have the phone stable, you will get more consistency in your art and even audio (assuming you stay in the same spot as well). Video quality is also improved by the stability for obvious reasons – and you can get the extension attached to the other iSteady gimbal modules like iSteady Pro for recording. It will give you a benefit of recording from your Camera plus from your smartphone as well. This is a must needed accessory for action camera stabilizers. This smartphone holder is suitable for every major brand of smartphone out there. <br>
						Gimbal:<br>
						1.	Reasons to use a gimbal in a camera<br>
						Ads Shake free motion to your camera<br>
						Before the introduction of gimbals, camera users move around their camera on a tripod or by holding in their hands. Now, they can actually take their cameras through anything and anywhere with the help of gimbals, due to their compact designing, gimbals can be carried and can make steady movement shots very easily that would otherwise require massive large camera rigs and equipment’s.<br>
						Gives option for a wide variety of motions<br>
						You may take lots of time to set up a shot more particularly when various movement tools are required in it. But a Gimbal allows videographers and photographers to shoot similar shots in much less time as it allows them to move their camera easily in different ways without wasting their time and efforts.</p>
						<blockquote class="quote-post">
				            <p>
				            	Saves Energy <br>
								The lightweight of a gimbal saves your energy and give you total freedom to move as you want. It makes the operation of the camera much more comfortable and relax than handling other stabilization options which often leave you with a sore arm due to their heavy weight.<br>

				            </p>
				        </blockquote>
				        <p>
				        	2.	How does a camera gimbal work?<br>
							Backed by algorithms and gyroscopes, gimbals pick up on any unwanted jolts or bumps in your footage – and then stabilise those moments with the help of inbuilt motors.<br>
							The popular 3-axis gimbals use Inertial Measurement Units (IMUs) to detect movement, and then adjust the gimbal on each axis to balance your camera and keep it still as you move through the scene to shoot . The best part? The gimbal’s built-in computer can determine which movements are intentional (and which ones aren’t), so your panning scenes and sweeping views again and again turn out the way you wanted them.<br>
							 Thanks to this technology, 3-axis gimbals can create a cinematic view yet dreamy effect, where the camera appears to be floating through the air all time.<br>
							 Finally, gimbal motors are normally brushless. This means they’re very silent – which is key to the quality of your finished work without any error.<br>
							On a budget? Though 3-axis gimbals offer the smoothest video, they’re the best investment for your creative work. If you’re looking for a cheaper alternative, go for a 1-axis or 2-axis gimbal at start before upgrading yourself.<br> 

				        </p>
